TOOL_EVENT {"run_id": "r-6d5f0a0ec8de-1", "task_id": "retry-q2-t0413-t0001", "trace_id": "retry-q2-t0413-trace0001", "member_id": "s1-r1-m02", "planned_activity_id": "retry-q2-t0413-p0001", "phase_id": "phase2", "workday": 3, "simulation_time": "2025-05-07T09:00:00.009000+08:00", "step": 1, "workflow_id": "software_feature", "call_id": "tool-19ba8f1072fc415192951486e1ee5b85", "provider_call_id": "call_b48874c0124242d9a8037082", "from_state": "issue_created", "tool_name": "design_document", "requested_tool_name": "design_document", "event_type": "tool_call", "to_state": "designed", "arguments": {"content": "Design Document: Arithmetic Mean Feature (compute / mean_values)\n\n1. Overview\nImplement a small Python feature that computes the arithmetic mean of two finite numeric inputs. The public entry point is compute(a, b) in src/main.py, which must return the arithmetic mean, e.g. compute(2, 3) == 2.5. The core math lives in mean_values(a, b) in src/helper.py, and src/main.py imports and uses it. A unittest in tests/test_compute.py verifies the required behavior.\n\n2. Requirements\n- R1: compute(a, b) defined in src/main.py returns the arithmetic mean of two finite numeric inputs.\n- R2: compute(2, 3) must return 2.5.\n- R3: Zero, negative and fractional values must be supported (e.g. compute(-2, 2) == 0.0, compute(0.5, 0.25) == 0.375).\n- R4: mean_values(a, b) implemented in src/helper.py; src/main.py imports and uses it (no duplicated math in main).\n- R5: tests/test_compute.py contains a unittest checking compute(2, 3) == 2.5.\n- R6: All three files required: src/main.py, src/helper.py, tests/test_compute.py.\n- R7: Validation: run checks/public_check.py with /opt/anaconda3/bin/python and build with /opt/anaconda3/bin/python -m compileall -q src.\n\n3. Design\n3.1 src/helper.py\n- Define mean_values(a, b):\n  - Validate that both a and b are numeric (int or float, excluding bool) and finite (math.isfinite). Raise TypeError for non-numeric inputs and ValueError for non-finite values (NaN, +/-inf).\n  - Return (a + b) / 2 as a float. Using float() conversion guarantees fractional results (2.5 for compute(2, 3)) even for int inputs.\n- No side effects; pure function.\n\n3.2 src/main.py\n- Import mean_values from helper. To make the import robust both when run as a module and when src is on sys.path (as checks/tests typically do), use a try/except import: first try a relative-style package import (from .helper import mean_values), falling back to a direct import (from helper import mean_values) when src is the working directory / on sys.path.\n- Define compute(a, b) as a thin delegation: return mean_values(a, b). This satisfies R4 (main uses the helper) and keeps a single source of truth for the math.\n- Expose __all__ = [\"compute\"].\n- No top-level executable behavior required; keep the module import-safe.\n\n3.3 tests/test_compute.py\n- Use unittest. Import compute from src.main by inserting the workspace root into sys.path (os.path + sys.path manipulation relative to the test file location) so the test runs with plain `python -m unittest` or pytest from the workspace root.\n- Test cases:\n  - test_basic: compute(2, 3) == 2.5 (required by R5).\n  - test_zero: compute(0, 0) == 0.0 and compute(-4, 4) == 0.0 (zero support).\n  - test_negative: compute(-2, -4) == -3.0 (negative support).\n  - test_fractional: compute(0.5, 0.25) == 0.375 (fractional support).\n  - test_returns_float: result is a float instance.\n- Guard against accidental non-finite inputs in tests is not needed; tests only use finite values.\n\n4. Data flow\ncompute(a, b) [src/main.py] -> mean_values(a, b) [src/helper.py] -> validated, finite-checked -> (a + b) / 2 -> float result returned to caller.\n\n5. Error handling\n- Non-numeric input (str, None, list, bool): TypeError from mean_values.\n- Non-finite numeric input (float('nan'), float('inf')): ValueError from mean_values.\n- compute propagates these errors unchanged; no silent coercion.\n\n6. Validation plan\n- Phase test_run: run the public behavioral check /opt/anaconda3/bin/python checks/public_check.py (must pass).\n- Phase build: /opt/anaconda3/bin/python -m compileall -q src (bytecode compilation of src must succeed).\n- Local simulated review and approval phases afterwards.\n\n7. Out of scope\n- Means of more than two values, weighted means, statistics module usage, packaging (build/pip), and any CLI entry point.", "input_artifact_id": "d752812663f5"}, "result": {"tool_name": "design_document", "message": "design document created", "output_artifact_ids": ["8b4f80c7acb9"], "output_paths": ["artifacts/8b4f80c7acb9.md"], "evidence_level": "simulation_only", "business_status": "not_measured", "payload": "Design Document: Arithmetic Mean Feature (compute / mean_values)\n\n1. Overview\nImplement a small Python feature that computes the arithmetic mean of two finite numeric inputs. The public entry point is compute(a, b) in src/main.py, which must return the arithmetic mean, e.g. compute(2, 3) == 2.5. The core math lives in mean_values(a, b) in src/helper.py, and src/main.py imports and uses it. A unittest in tests/test_compute.py verifies the required behavior.\n\n2. Requirements\n- R1: compute(a, b) defined in src/main.py returns the arithmetic mean of two finite numeric inputs.\n- R2: compute(2, 3) must return 2.5.\n- R3: Zero, negative and fractional values must be supported (e.g. compute(-2, 2) == 0.0, compute(0.5, 0.25) == 0.375).\n- R4: mean_values(a, b) implemented in src/helper.py; src/main.py imports and uses it (no duplicated math in main).\n- R5: tests/test_compute.py contains a unittest checking compute(2, 3) == 2.5.\n- R6: All three files required: src/main.py, src/helper.py, tests/test_compute.py.\n- R7: Validation: run checks/public_check.py with /opt/anaconda3/bin/python and build with /opt/anaconda3/bin/python -m compileall -q src.\n\n3. Design\n3.1 src/helper.py\n- Define mean_values(a, b):\n  - Validate that both a and b are numeric (int or float, excluding bool) and finite (math.isfinite). Raise TypeError for non-numeric inputs and ValueError for non-finite values (NaN, +/-inf).\n  - Return (a + b) / 2 as a float. Using float() conversion guarantees fractional results (2.5 for compute(2, 3)) even for int inputs.\n- No side effects; pure function.\n\n3.2 src/main.py\n- Import mean_values from helper. To make the import robust both when run as a module and when src is on sys.path (as checks/tests typically do), use a try/except import: first try a relative-style package import (from .helper import mean_values), falling back to a direct import (from helper import mean_values) when src is the working directory / on sys.path.\n- Define compute(a, b) as a thin delegation: return mean_values(a, b). This satisfies R4 (main uses the helper) and keeps a single source of truth for the math.\n- Expose __all__ = [\"compute\"].\n- No top-level executable behavior required; keep the module import-safe.\n\n3.3 tests/test_compute.py\n- Use unittest. Import compute from src.main by inserting the workspace root into sys.path (os.path + sys.path manipulation relative to the test file location) so the test runs with plain `python -m unittest` or pytest from the workspace root.\n- Test cases:\n  - test_basic: compute(2, 3) == 2.5 (required by R5).\n  - test_zero: compute(0, 0) == 0.0 and compute(-4, 4) == 0.0 (zero support).\n  - test_negative: compute(-2, -4) == -3.0 (negative support).\n  - test_fractional: compute(0.5, 0.25) == 0.375 (fractional support).\n  - test_returns_float: result is a float instance.\n- Guard against accidental non-finite inputs in tests is not needed; tests only use finite values.\n\n4. Data flow\ncompute(a, b) [src/main.py] -> mean_values(a, b) [src/helper.py] -> validated, finite-checked -> (a + b) / 2 -> float result returned to caller.\n\n5. Error handling\n- Non-numeric input (str, None, list, bool): TypeError from mean_values.\n- Non-finite numeric input (float('nan'), float('inf')): ValueError from mean_values.\n- compute propagates these errors unchanged; no silent coercion.\n\n6. Validation plan\n- Phase test_run: run the public behavioral check /opt/anaconda3/bin/python checks/public_check.py (must pass).\n- Phase build: /opt/anaconda3/bin/python -m compileall -q src (bytecode compilation of src must succeed).\n- Local simulated review and approval phases afterwards.\n\n7. Out of scope\n- Means of more than two values, weighted means, statistics module usage, packaging (build/pip), and any CLI entry point.", "output_paths_kind": "read_only_capture_evidence", "business_path": null, "workspace": "/Users/ayang/Desktop/project_work/Chimera/docs/dataset-evaluation/reports/stage2/full-from-scratch-20261015/q2-q3/failure-rerun-20261016/tasks/q2-t0413/study/workspaces/retry-q2-t0413-t0001", "execution_environment": {"cwd": "/Users/ayang/Desktop/project_work/Chimera/docs/dataset-evaluation/reports/stage2/full-from-scratch-20261015/q2-q3/failure-rerun-20261016/tasks/q2-t0413/study/workspaces/retry-q2-t0413-t0001", "python_executable": "/opt/anaconda3/bin/python", "default_build_command": "/opt/anaconda3/bin/python -m compileall -q src", "build_kind": "python_bytecode_compilation", "optional_modules": {"pytest": true, "build": false, "pip": true, "setuptools": true}}}, "status": "success", "schema_version": "chimera-event/1", "event_seq": 9, "event_id": "765a2d085cd34692a6cebf0988018c10", "wall_time_utc": "2026-10-16T07:52:14.322804+00:00", "simulation_clock_kind": "task_start_plus_logical_order"}
